{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"112","artifact_type":"architect","name":"architect-provenance-registry@sha256:3f927d00f8153abe06fd2e793bc11d853228c25cc6985705a2bd7dc009e7dd4b"},"owner_reference":"112","content":"\n# Architecture Spec: Rule Provenance \u2014 Staleness Detection, Not Fabrication Detection\n\n**Backlog item:** #112 \u2014 \"Hardcoded constant sets go stale silently \u2014 four live instances, and the designed fix was never built\"\n**Inputs read:** `docs/design-rule-provenance-registry.md`, `docs/registry-schema-examples.md`, `packages/skilllint/limits.py`, `packages/skilllint/rules/hk_series.py`, `packages/skilllint/rule_registry.py`, `packages/skilllint/schemas/claude_code/v1.json`, `packages/skilllint/schemas/agentskills_io/v1.json`, `packages/skilllint/schemas/_loader.py`, `packages/skilllint/_spec_constants.py`, `scripts/fetch_spec_schema.py`, `scripts/fetch_platform_docs.py`\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n\nIssue #112 names one defect with four live instances: a rule hardcodes an\nenumerated/field set, upstream documentation moves on, and skilllint has no\nmechanism to notice. `docs/design-rule-provenance-registry.md` already\ndesigned the fix \u2014 a Rule \u2192 Claim \u2192 Authority \u2192 Extraction \u2192 Comparison \u2192\nUpdate pipeline \u2014 but `packages/skilllint/schemas/provenance-registry.json`\nwas never built.\n\nThis spec **adopts that pipeline's shape** (registry schema, 5-stage flow,\nrisk-tiered auto-apply) with three corrections forced by what actually\nhappened in #112:\n\n1. **The design doc's own problem statement is wrong.** It claims 12 of\n   HK002's 22 event names have \"no traceable vendor source.\" All 12 appear\n   verbatim in both the 2026-03-23 and 2026-08-28 vendor captures \u2014 the audit\n   ran against a stale local cache. **Every one of the four #112 instances is\n   a staleness failure (upstream added, we didn't), not a fabrication\n   failure (we invented, upstream never had).** A pipeline built to catch\n   invention only would not have caught any of them. This spec makes\n   **live-fetch-and-compare** the enforcement mechanism (\u00a72, ADR-3), not\n   diffing against a possibly-stale local cache.\n2. **Stage 3 (Extract) does not need an LLM for the known instances.** HK002,\n   HK003, PL006, and NR001 are all markdown tables or bullet lists with fixed\n   shape. This spec makes mechanical (marko-AST) extraction the default per\n   claim and demotes the design doc's two-pass LLM extraction to an opt-in\n   fallback strategy for claims a mechanical extractor can't parse (ADR-2).\n3. **`RuleAuthority.reference: str | None` is replaced by a mandatory\n   discriminated union**, not by making `reference` required. SL001 (symlink\n   hygiene) and LK001 (link validity) have no vendor source and must not be\n   forced to invent one \u2014 that is the exact failure that got AS007 deleted.\n   Every rule now declares itself `SourcedAuthority` or `OpinionAuthority`;\n   both are honest, only one is drift-checkable (\u00a76, ADR-4).\n\nMigration order (\u00a710) ships value on the first PR: HK002 alone (pure\nadditions, zero risk, fixes 9 false positives) validates the schema \u2192\ngenerated-constants pipeline before HK003, PL006, and NR001 follow the same\nshape. NR001 \u2014 the one instance with both removals *and* an unreachable-code\nbug \u2014 goes last, once the risk-tiering machinery exists to handle it safely.\n\n## 2. Architecture Overview\n\n### 2.1 System Context\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    VENDOR[\"Vendor docs\\ncode.claude.com/docs\\nagentskills.io\\nschemastore.org\"]\n    CONTRIB[\"skilllint contributor\"]\n    CRON[\"Scheduled CI\\nprovenance-drift.yml\"]\n    PRGATE[\"PR CI\\nregistry-consistency.yml\"]\n    REG[(\"provenance-registry.json\\nopinion-catalog.json\")]\n    SCHEMA[(\"schemas/*/v1.json\\nfile_types + enums\")]\n    CONST[\"_generated_constants.py\"]\n    RULES[\"Rule modules\\nHK002 HK003 PL006 NR001 ...\"]\n    PLUGIN[\"Plugin under lint\"]\n\n    VENDOR -- \"fetch (live, no cache trust)\" --> CRON\n    CRON -- \"extract + compare\" --> REG\n    REG -- \"assertion_location\" --> SCHEMA\n    SCHEMA -- \"generate\" --> CONST\n    CONST -- \"import\" --> RULES\n    RULES -- \"validates\" --> PLUGIN\n    CONTRIB -- \"author claim / rule\" --> REG\n    REG -- \"self-consistency, no network\" --> PRGATE\n    CRON -- \"opens PR: additions auto,\\nremovals flagged\" --> CONTRIB\n```\n\n### 2.2 Containers\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Registry[\"Registry (data, versioned in git)\"]\n        R1[\"provenance-registry.json\\nsourced, drift-checkable claims\"]\n        R2[\"opinion-catalog.json\\nno vendor source, by design\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Boundary[\"packages/skilllint/provenance.py (new, boundary module)\"]\n        B1[\"parse_registry()\\nparse_opinion_catalog()\"]\n        B2[\"Pydantic models:\\nClaimEntry, SourcedAuthority,\\nOpinionAuthority, RiskTier\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph RuntimeCore[\"Typed core (existing package, runtime path)\"]\n        C1[\"rule_registry.py\\n@skilllint_rule(authority=...)\\nmandatory Sourced|Opinion\"]\n        C2[\"_generated_constants.py\\ngenerated, not hand-edited\"]\n        C3[\"rules/hk_series.py, pl_series.py,\\nnr_series.py \u2014 import constants\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Pipeline[\"scripts/ (dev + CI automation, PEP 723, not shipped)\"]\n        S1[\"fetch_platform_docs.py (existing, unchanged)\"]\n        S2[\"reduce_vendor_sections.py\\nmarko anchor extraction\"]\n        S3[\"extract_claims.py\\nmechanical per claim_type,\\nLLM two-pass fallback\"]\n        S4[\"compare_claims.py\\nJSON diff + risk tier\"]\n        S5[\"update_claims.py\\nschema + x-audited + drift report\"]\n        S6[\"generate_constants.py\\nschema enums -> _generated_constants.py\"]\n        S7[\"check_registry_consistency.py\\nNEW \u2014 no network, PR gate\"]\n    end\n\n    R1 --> B1\n    R2 --> B1\n    B1 --> C1\n    S1 --> S2 --> S3 --> S4 --> S5\n    R1 -.anchor selectors / extraction config.-> S2\n    R1 -.assertion_location.-> S4\n    S5 --> Registry\n    S5 --> S6 --> C2 --> C3\n    R1 --> S7\n    C1 --> S7\n    C2 --> S7\n```\n\n## 3. Technology Stack\n\nNo new dependency. `pydantic>=2.12.5`, `marko>=2.2.2`, `httpx>=0.28.1`, and\n`msgspec>=0.20.0` are already in `pyproject.toml` and already do everything\nthis design needs:\n\n| Need | Choice | Why not something else |\n|---|---|---|\n| Registry/catalog data model | Pydantic `BaseModel` with discriminated unions (`Field(discriminator=\"kind\")`) | Already the project's boundary standard (`rule_registry.py` uses `BaseModel` today). `msgspec` is faster but the project already draws the line at Pydantic for structured config/boundary models \u2014 no reason to introduce a second validation library for one new file. |\n| Vendor markdown parsing | `marko` with `gfm`+`toc` extensions | Already a dependency, already the design doc's choice, table/heading AST is exactly what mechanical extraction needs. |\n| Live doc fetch | `httpx` via existing `scripts/fetch_platform_docs.py` | Reused unchanged \u2014 no new fetch path. |\n| Constants generation | Plain Python codegen (`scripts/generate_constants.py`), same shape as `scripts/fetch_spec_schema.py` \u2192 `_spec_constants.py` | Direct precedent already in the repo; no templating engine needed for a `frozenset[str]` declaration. |\n| LLM extraction fallback (opt-in, not default) | Deferred \u2014 no client wired in this slice | None of the four #112 instances need it (\u00a79, ADR-2). Specifying a provider now would be an invented dependency for a code path this slice doesn't exercise. |\n\n## 4. Component Design\n\n### 4.1 `packages/skilllint/provenance.py` (new \u2014 typed-core boundary module)\n\nBoundary module per the project's typing policy: raw JSON in, validated\nPydantic models out, nothing downstream ever sees a dict.\n\n```python\ndef parse_registry(path: Path) -> ProvenanceRegistry: ...\ndef parse_opinion_catalog(path: Path) -> OpinionCatalog: ...\ndef load_registry() -> ProvenanceRegistry:\n    \"\"\"Loads the bundled packages/skilllint/schemas/provenance-registry.json\n    via importlib.resources, same access pattern as schemas/_loader.py.\"\"\"\n    ...\ndef load_opinion_catalog() -> OpinionCatalog: ...\n```\n\nConsumers: `rule_registry.py` (registry-completeness test, \u00a78), `scripts/compare_claims.py`\nand `scripts/check_registry_consistency.py` (pipeline), `skilllint rule <ID>`\nCLI command (renders authority + opinion rationale in rule docs \u2014 additive,\nno existing CLI contract changes).\n\n### 4.2 `packages/skilllint/rule_registry.py` (modified)\n\n`RuleAuthority` (currently `origin: str`, `reference: str | None`) is\nreplaced by the discriminated union defined in \u00a76. The `authority` parameter\non `@skilllint_rule` becomes **required, no default `None`** \u2014 a rule that\ndoes not pass `authority=` fails to register. `iter_authority_urls()` keeps\nits current signature and skip/log semantics for the `OpinionAuthority`\nbranch (no `reference` field exists there, so \"no reference\" is now\nstructural rather than a runtime `None` check \u2014 see \u00a76.3).\n\n`RuleEntry.authority` changes from `RuleAuthority | None` to the mandatory\nunion type \u2014 `RuleEntry` can no longer be constructed with a missing\nauthority, closing the exact hole that let 25 rules ship uncited.\n\n### 4.3 `scripts/reduce_vendor_sections.py` (new)\n\nUnchanged from the design doc's Stage 2 (\u00a75 of\n`docs/design-rule-provenance-registry.md`): marko-parse the vendor markdown\nfile named in a claim's `authority.vendor_file`, locate the heading matching\n`anchor_selector` by slug, extract the subtree through the next\nsame-or-higher heading, render back to markdown. Fallback to full-document\nwith a `selector_failed` warning when the anchor doesn't resolve \u2014 this\nwarning is itself a drift signal (the vendor doc reorganized) and gets\nsurfaced in the same PR the extraction stage produces.\n\nFollows the existing `scripts/fetch_platform_docs.py` PEP 723 shebang\npattern: standalone script, `[tool.uv.sources] skilllint = { path = \"..\" }`,\nimporting `provenance.py` for the registry models.\n\n### 4.4 `scripts/extract_claims.py` (new \u2014 mechanical-first)\n\nFor each affected claim, dispatch on `extraction.strategy`:\n\n```python\ndef extract_table_column(section_ast, column: str) -> list[str]: ...\ndef extract_list_items(section_ast) -> list[str]: ...\ndef extract_code_fence(section_ast, lang: str) -> str: ...\ndef extract_llm_two_pass(section_markdown: str, prompt_template: str, output_schema: dict) -> object: ...\n```\n\n`extract_table_column` and `extract_list_items` walk the marko AST directly\n(`Table.head`, `Table.children[1:]`, `TableCell.inline_body`; `List` /\n`ListItem` children) \u2014 this covers all four #112 instances (HK002: bullet\nlist of event names or a table column; HK003: `type` column of the Common\nfields table plus a `required-field-by-type` table; PL006: bullet list of\nroot keys; NR001: bullet list of the six built-in subagent names). No LLM\ncall, no network beyond the fetch already done in Stage 1, deterministic\noutput \u2014 this is what makes the claim testable with a committed fixture\n(\u00a78) instead of a live network call.\n\n`extract_llm_two_pass` is the design doc's original two-pass-agreement\nmechanism, kept as a named strategy for claims where no mechanical extractor\ncan locate a stable anchor (unstructured prose). **No claim in this slice\nsets `extraction.strategy: \"llm_two_pass\"`** \u2014 it exists as a documented\nextension point, not dead code shipped speculatively (see ADR-2 for why this\nisn't built further than the strategy enum right now).\n\n### 4.5 `scripts/compare_claims.py` (new \u2014 unchanged from design doc \u00a77)\n\nPer claim: load current value from `assertion_location`, diff against the\nfreshly extracted value, classify `MATCH | ADDED | REMOVED | CHANGED` per the\nrisk table in \u00a77. **Critical distinction from the design doc:** this stage\nalways compares the freshly extracted (live-fetched) value against the\n*currently recorded* schema/constants value \u2014 never against a previous local\n`.claude/vendor/` cache snapshot. Cache-diff (`.drift-pending.json`) stays\nscoped to the interactive SessionStart nudge (\u00a77.2); it is not part of the\nenforcement path, because a stale cache is exactly what produced the wrong\n\"12 events have no source\" claim this issue corrects.\n\n### 4.6 `scripts/update_claims.py` (new \u2014 unchanged from design doc \u00a78, risk-tiered)\n\nAuto-applies `ADDED` diffs to schema JSON `enums`/`file_types` and bumps\n`x-audited`; writes `REMOVED`/`CHANGED` diffs to a drift report for human\nreview; never auto-applies a removal. Runs inside a CI job that opens a PR \u2014\nnever pushes to `main` directly, regardless of risk tier (ADR-6).\n\n### 4.7 `scripts/generate_constants.py` (new)\n\nReads the `enums` section of every provider schema JSON, writes\n`packages/skilllint/_generated_constants.py` with one `frozenset[str]` (or\n`dict[str, str]` for `_REQUIRED_FIELD_BY_HOOK_TYPE`-shaped field sets) per\nclaim, in the same style as `_spec_constants.py`'s\n`# Auto-generated ... Do not edit manually` header. Idempotent: running it\ntwice with no schema change produces byte-identical output (required for the\nPR-gate consistency check in \u00a74.8 to be meaningful).\n\n### 4.8 `scripts/check_registry_consistency.py` (new \u2014 PR gate, no network)\n\nThree local, fast, deterministic checks, run on every PR touching\n`packages/skilllint/rules/`, `packages/skilllint/schemas/`, or\n`packages/skilllint/schemas/provenance-registry.json`:\n\n1. **Registry completeness:** every `RULE_REGISTRY` entry has a non-`None`\n   `authority` that is `SourcedAuthority` or `OpinionAuthority` (mechanical\n   walk, no I/O beyond importing the package).\n2. **Registry \u2194 schema resolution:** every claim's `assertion_location`\n   (`file` + `symbol`) resolves inside the named schema/constants file.\n3. **Constants freshness:** running `generate_constants.py` produces output\n   identical to the committed `_generated_constants.py` \u2014 catches \"someone\n   hand-edited the generated file\" or \"someone bumped the schema and forgot\n   to regenerate,\" independent of whether upstream vendor docs moved at all.\n\nThis is the check that runs on **every PR** (\u00a77.2 \u2014 the fast, network-free\ntier). It cannot detect upstream drift (it never fetches); it only detects\nrepo-internal inconsistency between rule code, registry, schema, and\ngenerated constants.\n\n### 4.9 Rule modules (`hk_series.py`, `pl_series.py`, `nr_series.py`) \u2014 modified\n\n`VALID_EVENT_TYPES`, `VALID_HOOK_TYPES`, `_REQUIRED_FIELD_BY_HOOK_TYPE`, the\nPL006 root-key allowlist, and `BUILTIN_AGENTS` become imports from\n`_generated_constants` instead of module-level literals. The rule\n*functions* (`check_hk002`, etc.) are unchanged \u2014 only where the set comes\nfrom moves. Each `@skilllint_rule(...)` decorator's `authority=` argument\nchanges from the current `{\"origin\": \"github.com/jamie-bitflight/claude_skills\"}`\n(which is itself an inaccurate self-referential citation for what are\nvendor-sourced rules \u2014 HK002/HK003 assert Claude Code's own hook contract,\nnot this project's) to a proper `SourcedAuthority` pointing at\n`code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks.md`.\n\n## 5. Data Architecture\n\n### 5.1 `packages/skilllint/schemas/provenance-registry.json`\n\nSame top-level shape as `docs/registry-schema-examples.md`, with the\nextraction block widened to support mechanical strategies:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\n  \"version\": \"1\",\n  \"claims\": {\n    \"HK002.valid_event_types\": {\n      \"rule_code\": \"HK002\",\n      \"claim_name\": \"valid_event_types\",\n      \"claim_type\": \"enum_set\",\n      \"authority\": {\n        \"vendor_file\": \".claude/vendor/sources/code-claude-com--docs--en--hooks-<timestamp>.md\",\n        \"anchor_selector\": \"#hook-events\",\n        \"authority_url\": \"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks.md\"\n      },\n      \"extraction\": {\n        \"strategy\": \"list_items\",\n        \"column\": null,\n        \"prompt_template\": null,\n        \"output_schema\": { \"type\": \"array\", \"items\": { \"type\": \"string\" } },\n        \"post_processing\": \"sort\"\n      },\n      \"assertion_location\": {\n        \"file\": \"packages/skilllint/schemas/claude_code/v1.json\",\n        \"symbol\": \"$.enums.valid_event_types.values\",\n        \"source_type\": \"schema_json_enum\"\n      },\n      \"x-audited\": { \"date\": \"2026-08-28\", \"source\": \"code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks.md\" }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nField-level changes from the design doc's schema (\u00a73 of the design doc):\n\n- `extraction.strategy: Literal[\"table_column\", \"list_items\", \"code_fence_json\", \"llm_two_pass\"]` \u2014\n  new, required. `prompt_template`/`output_schema` become required only when\n  `strategy == \"llm_two_pass\"` (Pydantic model-level validation, not a bare\n  Optional \u2014 see \u00a76.4).\n- `extraction.column: str | None` \u2014 new, required for `strategy ==\n  \"table_column\"`, names the table column header to extract (e.g. `\"type\"`\n  for HK003, `\"Field\"` for the required-field-by-type table).\n- `authority.vendor_file` now points at a `skilllint docs fetch`-cached path\n  under `.claude/vendor/sources/` (per this repo's own documented vendor\n  caching convention \u2014 see `CLAUDE.md` \"Vendor documentation cache\"),\n  **not** the ad-hoc `.claude/vendor/claude_code/...` tree the design doc\n  assumed. Both trees exist in this repo today; the registry standardizes on\n  the one with sidecar `.meta.json` integrity metadata, since that is what\n  makes \"verify this snapshot hasn't rotted\" (`skilllint docs verify`)\n  possible without a live fetch.\n\n### 5.2 `packages/skilllint/schemas/opinion-catalog.json`\n\nUnchanged shape from `docs/registry-schema-examples.md` \u00a7\"Opinion catalog\nentries.\" One entry per **claim** (not per rule \u2014 AS001's regex/hyphen rule\nand FM007's `disallowedTools` field stay here as partial-opinion entries\nexactly as the design doc specified), each carrying a mandatory `rationale`\nstring. No length minimum is imposed on `rationale` \u2014 inventing a numeric\nfloor here would repeat the exact defect this project's \"No invented\nconstraints\" rule exists to prevent. Non-emptiness is enforced structurally\n(`min_length=1`, i.e. \"field must be present and non-blank\"), not by a\nguessed quality threshold.\n\n### 5.3 `packages/skilllint/schemas/claude_code/v1.json` \u2014 `enums` extension\n\nAdditive: a top-level `enums` object alongside the existing `file_types`,\nusing the same `x-audited: {date, source}` shape the file already uses\nper-field. No structural change to `_loader.py` \u2014 `load_provider_schema()`\nalready returns the whole dict; `_generated_constants.py`'s generator reads\n`schema[\"enums\"]` directly.\n\n## 6. Type System Design\n\n### 6.1 Domain identifier inventory\n\n| Identifier | Representation | Boundary |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ClaimId` | `NewType(\"ClaimId\", str)`, pattern `^[A-Z]{2}\\d{3}\\.[a-z_]+$` | Validated at `ProvenanceRegistry` construction (Pydantic field validator on the `claims` dict keys) |\n| `RuleCode` | Already exists: `RuleEntry.id` pattern `^[A-Z]{2}\\d{3}$` | Unchanged, `rule_registry.py` |\n| `ClaimType` | `Literal[\"enum_set\", \"scalar\", \"pattern\", \"field_set\"]` | Registry model |\n| `ExtractionStrategy` | `Literal[\"table_column\", \"list_items\", \"code_fence_json\", \"llm_two_pass\"]` | Registry model |\n| `SourceType` | `Literal[\"python_constant\", \"schema_json_field\", \"schema_json_enum\"]` | Registry model |\n| `RiskTier` | `Literal[\"low\", \"high\"]` | `compare_claims.py` output model |\n| `ChangeVerdict` | `Literal[\"match\", \"added\", \"removed\", \"changed\"]` | `compare_claims.py` output model |\n\n### 6.2 Boundary validation map\n\n| Boundary | Mechanism | What crosses it typed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `provenance-registry.json` \u2192 `ProvenanceRegistry` | `provenance.py::parse_registry`, Pydantic `model_validate_json`, `model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")` | `ProvenanceRegistry` (dict of `ClaimEntry`) |\n| `opinion-catalog.json` \u2192 `OpinionCatalog` | `provenance.py::parse_opinion_catalog`, same pattern | `OpinionCatalog` (dict of `OpinionEntry`) |\n| Vendor markdown (raw text) \u2192 reduced AST subtree | `reduce_vendor_sections.py`, marko `Markdown.parse()` \u2014 this *is* the boundary; marko's node types are the typed internal representation, nothing raw crosses further | marko `Element` tree |\n| Extracted claim value (mechanical) \u2192 comparable value | `extract_claims.py` return types are the `output_schema`-shaped Python value (`list[str] | str | dict[str, str]`), not `Any` \u2014 each `extract_*` function has a concrete return annotation matching its strategy, no generic `object`/`Any` return | Concrete typed value per `claim_type` |\n| `@skilllint_rule(authority=...)` decorator argument (currently a raw `dict | None`) \u2192 `RuleAuthority` union | `rule_registry.py::skilllint_rule` \u2014 same boundary role it already has, now converting into the mandatory discriminated union instead of an optional flat model | `SourcedAuthority \\| OpinionAuthority` |\n\nEverything downstream of these five boundaries \u2014 the rule functions, the CLI,\n`compare_claims.py`'s diff logic \u2014 operates only on the typed models. No\n`Any`, no `cast()`, consistent with the project's typing policy.\n\n### 6.3 Type contract: `RuleAuthority` becomes a mandatory discriminated union\n\nThis directly answers issue #112 decision point 3.\n\n```python\nclass SourcedAuthority(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"A rule whose assertion is backed by an external vendor/spec source.\"\"\"\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")\n    kind: Literal[\"sourced\"] = \"sourced\"\n    origin: str                      # e.g. \"code.claude.com\"\n    reference: str                   # REQUIRED \u2014 absolute URL or root-relative path; no None\n    vendor_file: str | None = None   # cached snapshot path, if one exists (skilllint docs fetch)\n    last_verified: date | None = None\n\n\nclass OpinionAuthority(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"A rule with no vendor source, by design \u2014 a skilllint-specific check.\"\"\"\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")\n    kind: Literal[\"opinion\"] = \"opinion\"\n    rationale: str                   # REQUIRED \u2014 why this is a deliberate opinion, not a citation\n    superseded_by: str | None = None # points at a later ADR/issue if the opinion was revisited\n\n\nRuleAuthority = Annotated[SourcedAuthority | OpinionAuthority, Field(discriminator=\"kind\")]\n```\n\nWhy a union instead of \"just make `reference` required\": making `reference`\nrequired on the *existing single model* forces every rule \u2014 including\nSL001 (symlink target hygiene) and LK001 (markdown link validity), which\nhave no vendor source and never will \u2014 to either supply `reference=None`\n(status quo, meaningless) or fabricate a citation to satisfy the type\nchecker. The second path is exactly how AS007 shipped a citation to an\nanchor that never existed. `OpinionAuthority.rationale` gives those two\nrules (and any future ones) a slot that is honest *and* required: they must\nsay why they're a pure lint opinion, they must not pretend to cite a spec\nthey don't have.\n\n`model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")` on both variants makes the\nfailure mode structural rather than convention-based: a `SourcedAuthority`\nliterally has no `rationale` field to abuse, and an `OpinionAuthority`\nliterally has no `reference` field to fabricate into. `ty`/Pydantic reject\n`{\"kind\": \"opinion\", \"reference\": \"https://made-up.example\"}` at\nconstruction, not at code review.\n\n**Creation \u2192 validation \u2192 consumption \u2192 serialization for `RuleAuthority`:**\n\n1. **Creation:** a contributor writes `authority={\"kind\": \"sourced\", \"origin\": ..., \"reference\": ...}` or `authority={\"kind\": \"opinion\", \"rationale\": ...}` in the `@skilllint_rule(...)` call.\n2. **Validation:** `skilllint_rule()`'s decorator body constructs the union via `TypeAdapter(RuleAuthority).validate_python(authority)` \u2014 malformed input (missing discriminator, wrong kind's required field) raises at **import time**, i.e. `uv run pytest` fails the moment the module is imported, not at lint-time on some user's plugin.\n3. **Consumption:** `RuleEntry.authority: RuleAuthority` (no longer `| None`). `iter_authority_urls()` pattern-matches on `.kind` \u2014 the `SourcedAuthority` branch does the existing `urljoin` resolution (\u00a7 of `rule_registry.py`, unchanged), the `OpinionAuthority` branch is skipped (it has no URL, structurally, not by a `None` check).\n4. **Serialization:** `skilllint rule <ID>` CLI output renders `SourcedAuthority` as today (\"Source: {origin}{reference}\") and renders `OpinionAuthority` as a new \"Opinion \u2014 no vendor source: {rationale}\" line, so a user reading rule docs sees the honest label instead of nothing.\n\n### 6.4 Type contract: `ExtractionConfig` (registry claim entry)\n\n```python\nclass ExtractionConfig(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")\n    strategy: Literal[\"table_column\", \"list_items\", \"code_fence_json\", \"llm_two_pass\"]\n    column: str | None = None\n    prompt_template: str | None = None\n    output_schema: dict[str, object] | None = None\n    post_processing: Literal[\"sort\", \"unique\", \"lowercase\"] | None = None\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def _require_fields_for_strategy(self) -> Self:\n        \"\"\"table_column requires `column`; llm_two_pass requires\n        `prompt_template` and `output_schema`. Enforced here rather than by\n        four separate Optional fields nobody knows how to pair correctly.\"\"\"\n        ...\n```\n\nThis is the \"weak type audit\" catch for this slice: the design doc's\noriginal schema left `prompt_template`/`output_schema` unconditionally\npresent (`str`, non-optional) on every claim, which would have forced every\nmechanical-strategy claim (all four #112 instances) to carry a dead LLM\nprompt nobody runs. Making them conditionally required by `strategy` removes\nthat dead weight and gives `ty`/Pydantic something to actually check instead\nof a comment saying \"ignore this field for non-LLM claims.\"\n\n### 6.5 Weak type audit\n\n| Location | Risk | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|\n| `RuleEntry.fn: Any` (existing, `rule_registry.py`) | `Any` on the stored callable | **Out of scope for this change** \u2014 `Callable` objects registered via a decorator genuinely have heterogeneous signatures (frontmatter-only vs hooks-config vs filesystem-walking rules, per `hk_series.py`'s own docstring). Flagging it here so it isn't silently carried forward as precedent for *new* `Any` usage in this slice \u2014 none is introduced. |\n| Old `RuleAuthority.reference: str | None` | The exact defect this spec fixes | Replaced per \u00a76.3 \u2014 no `Any`, no silent `None`. |\n| `extraction.output_schema: dict[str, object]` (\u00a76.4) | Bounded `object`, not `Any` \u2014 JSON Schema fragments are genuinely heterogeneous by design (JSON Schema's own meta-schema is dict-shaped) and this field is inert unless `strategy == \"llm_two_pass\"`, which no claim in this slice sets | Acceptable: `dict[str, object]` over `Any` still forbids arbitrary attribute access; tightening further (e.g. a `JsonSchemaFragment` model) is deferred until an `llm_two_pass` claim actually exists to test it against \u2014 building it now would be speculative. |\n| `cast()` | None anywhere in this design | New code has no boundary narrowing that needs it; marko's typed node classes (`Table`, `Heading`, `ListItem`) are used via `isinstance` narrowing, not `cast`. |\n\n## 7. Drift Detection \u2014 Two Tiers, Neither Trusts a Cached Diff\n\nThis section is the direct answer to #112 decision point 2, corrected per\nthe coordinator's note: the mechanism must catch **staleness** (a stale\nsubset) as its primary case, since that is all four #112 instances are.\n\n### 7.1 Tier 1 \u2014 PR gate, no network, every PR (`check_registry_consistency.py`, \u00a74.8)\n\nRuns in the existing CI workflow (whichever one already runs `uv run pytest`\n/ `ruff` / `ty check` on PRs \u2014 this adds one more `uv run` step, not a new\nworkflow file, per this repo's \"no inline CI logic\" rule). Catches\nrepo-internal drift: rule code, registry, schema, and generated constants\ndisagreeing with each other. **Cannot** catch upstream drift \u2014 it never\nfetches. This is fast (< 1s, no I/O beyond the repo itself) and belongs on\nevery PR because it has zero flake risk.\n\n### 7.2 Tier 2 \u2014 scheduled, network, live-fetch-and-compare (`provenance-drift.yml`, new)\n\nA separate, scheduled (cron, e.g. weekly \u2014 an operational cadence, not a\nsourced spec constant, tunable without touching this design) GitHub Actions\nworkflow, plus `workflow_dispatch` for on-demand runs:\n\n1. `scripts/fetch_platform_docs.py` (existing, unchanged) \u2014 fresh checkout,\n   so this fetches from the actual internet, not from any persisted cache.\n   `.claude/vendor/` is gitignored; CI starts empty every run by\n   construction, which is a feature here, not a gap to work around: it\n   forces every scheduled run to be an honest current-state check instead of\n   an incremental diff against whatever a previous run happened to cache.\n2. `reduce_vendor_sections.py` \u2192 `extract_claims.py` (mechanical, per \u00a74.4)\n   \u2192 `compare_claims.py` \u2014 compares the value **just extracted from the live\n   doc** against the value **currently recorded in the schema**. This is the\n   correction: the design doc's Stage 1 `.drift-pending.json`\n   changed-file-list mechanism is *not* part of this comparison. It remains\n   useful for the interactive SessionStart hook nudge (`vendor-drift-check.cjs`,\n   unchanged) \u2014 \"your local cache looks stale, want to refresh?\" \u2014 a\n   convenience signal for humans, never the enforcement gate.\n3. `update_claims.py` risk-tiers the result (\u00a74.6/\u00a79): `ADDED`-only diffs\n   auto-generate a PR with the schema + generated-constants update already\n   applied (still a PR, never a direct push \u2014 ADR-6); any `REMOVED` or\n   `CHANGED` diff is written to a drift report and the workflow **fails**\n   (non-zero exit), forcing a human to open an issue/PR consciously rather\n   than silently landing a tightened constraint.\n\n### 7.3 Why this order of tiers\n\nRunning the network-dependent check on every PR would make an unrelated\ndocs-typo PR's merge gate depend on `code.claude.com` being reachable \u2014\nflaky and slow for zero benefit (a rule's constants don't change between two\ncommits an hour apart). Running it only on a schedule means the worst case\nis \"up to a week of staleness before detection,\" which is still infinitely\nbetter than #112's actual status quo (five months, per the AS007 note in\n`docs/registry-schema-examples.md`, and no floor on HK002/HK003/PL006/NR001\nsince nothing has ever run this check).\n\n## 8. Testing Architecture\n\n### 8.1 Deterministic fixtures replace the live cache\n\n`packages/skilllint/tests/fixtures/vendor_snapshots/` holds **committed**\nmarkdown snippets \u2014 frozen copies of the relevant vendor doc sections, not\n`.claude/vendor/` (gitignored, machine-local, exactly the thing that went\nstale and produced the wrong claim in the design doc). Each fixture is\nnamed `<claim_id>-<YYYYMMDD>.md` and is the \"cached remote document\" the\nbrief asks for a deterministic drift test against \u2014 pinned by being in git,\nnot by trusting whatever happens to be on disk.\n\n### 8.2 Constants-vs-docs contract test\n\nParametrized over every `ClaimEntry` in `provenance-registry.json` with\n`extraction.strategy != \"llm_two_pass\"` (i.e., every claim in this slice):\nrun `extract_claims.py`'s mechanical extractor against the claim's pinned\nfixture, assert the result equals the value currently recorded at\n`assertion_location`. This is the direct \"does our recorded constant match\nwhat the doc actually says\" test, and it never touches the network \u2014 the\nfixture *is* the doc, by construction.\n\n### 8.3 Drift-classification test (deterministic, no network)\n\nFor at least the HK002 case (pure-addition, matches the real #112 finding),\na fixture pair: `hk002-events-before.md` (the current 22-name state) and\n`hk002-events-after.md` (a synthetic doc with one more name than today's 22,\nstanding in for the real 31). `compare_claims.py` run against the pair must\nclassify the new name `ADDED` / risk `low`, and a second synthetic pair with\none name removed must classify `REMOVED` / risk `high`. This is how \"test\ndrift detection deterministically when the fixture is a cached remote\ndocument\" is satisfied without a live fetch anywhere in the test suite.\n\n### 8.4 Registry boundary tests (Hypothesis)\n\nPer the project's typing policy (Hypothesis available \u2192 property-test\nboundaries): `provenance.py::parse_registry` and `parse_opinion_catalog` get\nHypothesis strategies generating malformed discriminator combinations\n(`kind=\"opinion\"` with a `reference` key present, `kind=\"sourced\"` missing\n`reference`) and assert `ValidationError` \u2014 this is the property-based\nversion of the structural claim in \u00a76.3 (\"this cannot construct\"), run\nagainst the actual Pydantic model rather than argued about in prose.\n\n### 8.5 Registry completeness test (repo-wide, mechanical)\n\nOne test, no fixtures needed: import `skilllint.rule_registry`, walk\n`RULE_REGISTRY.values()`, assert every `entry.authority` is a\n`SourcedAuthority` or `OpinionAuthority` instance. This is the test that\nenforces the \u00a710 migration sweep incrementally \u2014 it can run in `warn`\n(collect-and-report) mode until the full 25-rule backfill lands, then flip\nto a hard assertion (see \u00a710, step 7).\n\n### 8.6 Coverage expectations\n\n`provenance.py` (boundary module): 95%+, the standard the project's typing\npolicy sets for boundary/validator code, plus the Hypothesis properties in\n\u00a78.4. `extract_claims.py`'s mechanical strategies: one fixture-backed test\nper `claim_type` (enum_set, field_set covered by the four #112 instances;\nscalar and pattern remain opinion/schema-only per \u00a75.2 and don't need a\nmechanical extractor in this slice). `compare_claims.py`: parametrized over\nall four `ChangeVerdict` values.\n\n## 9. Distribution Architecture\n\n- **Ships in the installed package** (`packages/skilllint/`): `provenance.py`,\n  the modified `rule_registry.py`, `_generated_constants.py`, the modified\n  rule modules, and the two new schema JSON files\n  (`provenance-registry.json`, `opinion-catalog.json`) as bundled resources\n  loaded via `importlib.resources` \u2014 same mechanism `schemas/_loader.py`\n  already uses for `agentskills_io/v1.json` etc. These are needed at\n  **runtime** by every `skilllint check` invocation and by `skilllint rule\n  <ID>`.\n- **Stays in `scripts/`, not shipped**: `reduce_vendor_sections.py`,\n  `extract_claims.py`, `compare_claims.py`, `update_claims.py`,\n  `generate_constants.py`, `check_registry_consistency.py`. These are\n  dev/CI automation, exactly the category `CLAUDE.md`'s test-layout section\n  already draws a line around for `scripts/fetch_platform_docs.py` and\n  `scripts/fetch_spec_schema.py`. Each follows the same PEP 723 shebang +\n  `[tool.uv.sources] skilllint = { path = \"..\" }` pattern already\n  established by those two scripts, so they can import `provenance.py` and\n  the schema loader without being part of the wheel.\n- No new CLI entry point. `skilllint rule <ID>` (existing) gains richer\n  output (\u00a76.3 point 4) but no new command; the pipeline scripts are invoked\n  by CI workflows and by contributors directly (`uv run scripts/compare_claims.py`),\n  not through the `skilllint` console script.\n\n## 10. Migration Path\n\nOrdered to ship value on the first PR and to put the riskiest instance\n(removals + a real code bug) last, once the risk-tiering machinery exists to\nhandle it safely.\n\n1. **Contract + empty registry.** Land `provenance.py`, the\n   `RuleAuthority` discriminated union in `rule_registry.py`, empty\n   `provenance-registry.json`/`opinion-catalog.json` (schema-valid, zero\n   claims), and Tier 1 consistency check (\u00a77.1) in **warn** mode. No rule\n   behavior changes. This is the gate everything else builds on, and it's\n   independently valuable: it stops the 26th uncited rule from shipping.\n2. **HK002.** Pure `ADDED` case (9 missing names, 0 removed) \u2014 lowest risk,\n   immediately fixes 9 real false positives reported in #112, and exercises\n   the full schema \u2192 `generate_constants.py` \u2192 rule-import chain end to end\n   before the harder instances lean on it.\n3. **HK003.** Second `enum_set` claim (adds `mcp_tool`) plus the first\n   `field_set` claim (`_REQUIRED_FIELD_BY_HOOK_TYPE` gains `mcp_tool` \u2192\n   `{\"server\", \"tool\"}` \u2014 note this is a *pair* of required fields, not a\n   single string, so the field_set claim's `assertion_location.symbol`\n   points at a `dict[str, list[str]]`, not `dict[str, str]` like the other\n   three hook types; the type contract in \u00a76 doesn't special-case this, it's\n   just a different `output_schema` shape for the same `table_column`\n   strategy).\n4. **PL006.** Third `field_set` claim (5 additions, 0 removals) \u2014 validates\n   the pattern generalizes past hooks.json to plugin manifests without new\n   registry schema changes.\n5. **NR001.** Deliberately last: this instance is **not** a pure data-drift\n   fix. `BUILTIN_AGENTS` needs both a removal-tier registry claim (the\n   frozenset has 29 names with no vendor backing at all, which land in\n   `opinion-catalog.json` only if a rationale can honestly be written for\n   each \u2014 most likely most of them are simply deleted, not catalogued as\n   opinions, since \"this machine's own plugin agent names\" is not a\n   `skilllint` design choice, it's leftover data) *and* a fix to the\n   documented always-false guard condition, which is a logic bug outside\n   this registry's scope and should be reviewed/fixed as ordinary code, with\n   the registry claim covering only the six real built-in names afterward.\n6. **Enable Tier 2 (scheduled drift check, \u00a77.2)** once all four claims are\n   registered \u2014 the first scheduled run should have full coverage rather\n   than partial, so its first output is a meaningful signal, not noise from\n   claims nobody wired in yet.\n7. **Backfill sweep.** The remaining ~21 rules currently shipping with no\n   citation get triaged into `SourcedAuthority` or `OpinionAuthority` (most,\n   per the design doc's own audit, are opinions \u2014 FM004, AS007's\n   already-deleted precedent, etc.). Once complete, flip the \u00a78.5\n   registry-completeness test from `warn` to a hard `assert`.\n\n## 11. Scalability Strategy\n\nThis is a low-volume, CI/dev-tooling system (dozens of claims, not\nthousands), so the relevant scalability concern is **cost and flakiness of\nnetwork calls**, not throughput:\n\n- Tier 1 (\u00a77.1) never touches the network \u2014 scales with repo size, not\n  vendor doc size, and runs on every PR without concern.\n- Tier 2 (\u00a77.2) fetches N vendor files where N is the number of distinct\n  `authority.vendor_file` values across all registered claims \u2014 bounded by\n  claim count, deduplicated by file (multiple claims sharing one vendor file,\n  e.g. both HK002 and HK003 reading `hooks.md`, fetch it once via the\n  existing `fetch_platform_docs.py` dedup, then extract multiple claims from\n  the one cached copy in that run).\n- Mechanical extraction (\u00a74.4, default strategy) is O(claims) AST walks over\n  already-small reduced sections (\u00a74.3's anchor-scoped subtree, not the full\n  vendor doc) \u2014 no batching or async needed at this scale. If `llm_two_pass`\n  claims are ever added, the design doc's existing two-pass-per-claim\n  sequential model is the right default (per-claim LLM calls are already\n  rare and section-scoped by Stage 2); parallelizing them across claims with\n  an `asyncio.Semaphore` (per this project's standard async CLI pattern)\n  is the natural extension **if and when** that path is actually exercised \u2014\n  not built speculatively now for zero current claims.\n\n---\n\n## Architectural Decisions\n\n**ADR-1: Sourced constants live in schema JSON + generated Python, not as\nPython-literal-plus-comment.**\nRejected keeping `limits.py`'s literal-plus-provenance-comment pattern as\nthe long-term home for *drift-checkable* constants: a comment is not\nmachine-comparable, which is the entire mechanism this issue needs.\nRejected making rules read `provenance-registry.json` directly at runtime:\nadds parsing cost and loses static frozenset visibility to `ty`/IDEs for no\nbenefit \u2014 the registry's job is authoring/drift-checking, not runtime\nlookup. Generation (`schemas/*/v1.json` \u2192 `_generated_constants.py`, exact\nprecedent: `fetch_spec_schema.py` \u2192 `_spec_constants.py`) gets both: JSON is\nthe diffable, comparable source of truth; the generated module is what rules\nimport, type-checked and IDE-visible like any other module constant.\n`limits.py`'s literal+comment pattern remains correct for **opinion**\nscalars/patterns that have no vendor doc to compare against (AS001's regex,\nSL001's checks) \u2014 those were never going to be machine-drift-checked and\nforcing them into the registry would misrepresent them as sourced.\n\n**ADR-2: Mechanical (marko AST) extraction is the default strategy;\nLLM two-pass is an opt-in fallback, not built further in this slice.**\nRejected LLM-first extraction as the design doc specified: all four #112\ninstances are fixed-shape markdown tables/lists with stable anchors \u2014 an LLM\ncall adds cost, latency, and (even at two-pass agreement) residual\nnondeterminism to a CI gate that should be boring and reliable. Rejected\nremoving the LLM path entirely: some future claim may live in genuinely\nunstructured prose where no stable table/list anchor exists, and the design\ndoc's two-pass-agreement mechanism is a reasonable answer for that case when\nit arrives. Chosen: `extraction.strategy` as a per-claim enum, mechanical\nstrategies fully specified and used by all four current claims, `llm_two_pass`\nspecified as a contract (\u00a76.4) but not wired to a concrete LLM client until a\nreal claim needs it \u2014 building the client now would be speculative\ninfrastructure for zero current callers.\n\n**ADR-3: CI drift enforcement compares against a live fetch every run, never\nagainst a diffed local cache.**\nThis is the direct fix for the coordinator's correction. Rejected reusing\n`.drift-pending.json` (design doc's original Stage 1 trigger) as the\nenforcement signal: `.claude/vendor/` is gitignored, so CI has no\npersistent cache lineage to diff against, and the one time this exact \"diff\nagainst a cache\" approach was used for real (the audit behind the design\ndoc's problem statement), it produced a false claim that 12 of HK002's event\nnames had no source. Chosen: every Tier 2 run fetches fresh and compares the\nextracted value directly against the currently-recorded schema value (\u00a77.2)\n\u2014 \"is what we have right now consistent with what upstream says right now,\"\nwith no dependency on what any previous run happened to have cached.\n`.drift-pending.json` keeps a narrower, honest role: a local convenience\nsignal for the SessionStart hook, never a correctness gate.\n\n**ADR-4: `RuleAuthority` is a mandatory discriminated union\n(`SourcedAuthority | OpinionAuthority`), not a required `reference: str`.**\nRejected making `reference` non-optional on the existing single model: that\nforces SL001 and LK001 \u2014 sound rules with genuinely no vendor source \u2014 to\neither leave it meaninglessly empty or fabricate a citation, which is\nliterally the AS007 failure mode (a citation pointing at an anchor that\nnever existed). Rejected leaving `reference: str | None` as-is: that\noptionality is the documented root cause of 25 rules shipping with no\ncitation at all \u2014 \"optional\" reads as \"forgot it,\" not \"deliberately has\nnone.\" Chosen: two structurally distinct, both-mandatory variants (\u00a76.3),\nso every rule states its own kind honestly and the two failure modes (no\ncitation vs. fabricated citation) both become impossible by construction\nrather than by convention.\n\n**ADR-5: Two-tier CI (network-free PR gate + scheduled live-fetch gate),\nnot one combined check on every PR.**\nRejected a single check running on every PR: it would make merges depend on\n`code.claude.com`/`agentskills.io` reachability for changes that have\nnothing to do with vendor docs, trading a fast reliable gate for a slow\nflaky one. Chosen: Tier 1 (\u00a77.1, every PR, no network, catches repo-internal\ninconsistency) and Tier 2 (\u00a77.2, scheduled, network, catches upstream\nstaleness) as separate workflows with separate failure semantics \u2014 a\ndocs-typo PR never blocks on vendor reachability, and vendor staleness never\nwaits for the next unrelated PR to surface.\n\n**ADR-6: Auto-applied diffs land as an opened PR, never a direct commit to\n`main`, at any risk tier.**\nRejected auto-committing `ADDED`-only (low-risk) diffs straight to `main`\neven though the design doc's risk table calls additions \"auto-apply\": \"low\nrisk\" describes the *data* change, not the wisdom of an unreviewed bot\ncommit to a linter's source of truth. Chosen: `update_claims.py` always\nproduces a PR (git branch + PR body containing the drift report); \"auto\"\nmeans \"no human decision required before a PR exists,\" not \"no human review\nbefore it merges.\" This costs one PR-review click per drift event and buys\nback the ability to catch a bad extraction (e.g. a mis-parsed table column)\nbefore it's live, which a direct push would not.\n"}